\par \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 While in the ICU, they almost had to insert draining tubes in my head because my spinal fluid wasn\hich\f37 \rquote \loch\f37 t draining properly. I remember one instance where I couldn\hich\f37 \rquote \loch\f37 
t move my head or eyes. It was as if my entire body had tensed up and frozen, even my eyes. I co\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 uld see and hear EVERYTHING, but I just couldn\hich\f37 \rquote \loch\f37 
t move. The doctors immediately sent me for a CT scan. Everything was okay. A few hours passed by, and I unfroze, returning to my new normal: my paralyzed self.
\par 
\par \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 When I regained consciousness and could think \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 
straight, I started realizing some things. I was going to have to live like this, at least in the short term, and possibly the long term. I could feel everything on my left side, but I just couldn\hich\f37 \rquote \loch\f37 
t move anything. This realization hit me hard and ran deep\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 \hich\f37  within me. I thought, \'93\loch\f37 \hich\f37 How long do I have to do this?\'94\loch\f37 
 In the hospital, it was easy because I had everything done for me, but at home, I was, for the most part, on my own.
\par 
\par \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 My wife was pregnant with our little boy when this tragic event occurred. She staye\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 
d with me the entire time, only going home to shower every few days. She would sit in the chair next to me, holding my paralyzed arm, and keeping my fingers straight so they wouldn\hich\f37 \rquote \loch\f37 
t curl up and get stuck that way. I had seen many stroke survivors walking\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37  around with one arm all curled up, so that meant a lot to me.
\par 
\par \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 Since this stroke was so sudden and hectic, everyone was going crazy. My parents were furious because they couldn\hich\f37 \rquote \loch\f37 t come back to see me. My blood pressure wasn\hich\f37 \rquote \loch\f37 
t stable, so my wife tried to kee\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 p the visitors down, because she didn\hich\f37 \rquote \loch\f37 
t want to see it rise due to company. There were a few fights about that. Eventually, my blood pressure went down, so family and friends were allowed to come see me. It felt good, being able to see people and knowing \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 
I could still recognize them. My brain was still there. That was a moral victory. Also, knowing I had such a huge support system, gave me confidence towards the trying time I was about to face.
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\sa200\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af37\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \f37\fs22\lang9\langfe1033\kerning0\langnp9\insrsid10578587 
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\sa200\sl276\slmult1\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af37\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \f37\fs22\lang9\langfe1033\kerning0\langnp9\insrsid10578587 
\par \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 Most stroke recovery comes in the first few days to months, s\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 
o getting therapy fast was imperative. Therapists started coming in after I got out of the ICU. Their goal was just to see if I could sit up and balance myself and see if i had any movement at all in my affected leg. They would wrap the gait belt around m
\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 
e, maneuver my body, and lift me up. The entire left side of my body was limp, so almost the entirety of the work was on them. Also, speech therapy would come and give me tests such. I was having trouble swallowing, and it sounded like I was holding my br
\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 ea\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 
th when I would talk. They gave me a breathing machine I would have to practice blowing into for a certain amount of time. I also had some exercises where I would practice enunciating words.
\par 
\par \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 After a few days of that, the therapists decided to try and get me to stand up and bear at least a little weight on my left leg. It was one of the hardest things I\hich\f37 \rquote \loch\f37 
ve ever tried. Imagine being able to feel everything on one side of your body, yet not bein\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 g able to move it. That was so frustrating. After a few days, I w\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 
as able to lift my left leg a few inches off of the bed. That was a major achievement because I could tell some of my brain was making new connections, and my body was coming back. I spent days after that doing those simple, yet incredibly hard, elevation
\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37  exercises.
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\sa200\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af37\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \f37\fs22\lang9\langfe1033\kerning0\langnp9\insrsid10578587 
\par 
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\sa200\sl276\slmult1\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af37\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \f37\fs22\lang9\langfe1033\kerning0\langnp9\insrsid10578587 
\par 
\par \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 
When I was first transferred into rehab, I was still pretty much out of it and hopped up on pain medication, but the part of me that was still there was pretty nervous. I still had lots of staples in my head, and wore a beanie, most of the t
\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 ime, to protect them and also to try and hide them from people. I\hich\f37 \rquote \loch\f37 ve always had a problem with anxiety and worrying about what people thought about me. It\hich\f37 \rquote \loch\f37 
s pretty embarrassing going in front of people for the first time after major surgery, because you d\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 on\hich\f37 \rquote \loch\f37 t know what people are thinking or saying about you behind your back.
\par 
\par \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 The rehabilitation facility also allowed my wife to live with me. I really needed that at this stage in my life, because I was all confused, and just needed her by my side, although w\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 
e had been having problems. I started off in a wheelchair full time, just trying to bear weight. We would practice weight bearing, pivoting, balance exercises, and various exercises just trying to get me out of my wheelchair. In my wheelchair, we would do
\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 
 heel and toe raises, leg lifts, and things designed to strengthen my leg, foot, and ankle. Those were very difficult because I was in a very dark place. I was basically still paralyzed on my left side, and felt there was no way out. I was trapped in my o
\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 wn\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37  body.
\par 
\par \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 After therapy, I could go back to my room and watch TV with my wife. She would help feed me, and make sure I would drink all of my protein shakes, because I wasn\hich\f37 \rquote \loch\f37 
t eating very good. I spent about a month in rehab. That\hich\f37 \rquote \loch\f37 s when I met Michael. Michael was a \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 tall man, about 6\hich\f37 \rquote \loch\f37 \hich\f37 4", and 160 pounds, soaking wet. I thought, 
\'93\loch\f37 \hich\f37 how in the hell is this skinny guy going to lift me up and help me recover?\'94\loch\f37  Keep in mind I\hich\f37 \rquote \loch\f37 m 6'1", and lucky if the scale shows 260 pounds after starving myself for week. I could tell h
\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 
e was on the newer side of therapy, because it seemed every five seconds he was asking another therapist a question about me or seeking guidance to assure himself he was doing the best thing to maximize my recovery. I couldn't hate on that, because I coul
\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 d tell he cared, and I wanted to maximize my recovery, as well.  I\hich\f37 \rquote \loch\f37 
ll give you this, he was definitely dedicated and passionate about his craft. We worked and worked and worked. He would somehow support my left side and ankle, and we would walk around the\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37  g
\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 ym. Keep in mind, I had no AFO on during this time.  Every time I would try and put weight on my affected side, it would turn, so we laid off weight bearing too much.
\par \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 
As the days in the rehabilitation center passed, the time came for me to return home. Fear gripped me as I faced the uncertainty of how I would manage in my own house. Thankfully, my home was a single-story building, eliminating the challenge of stairs. M
\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 y father, foreseeing the difficulties I would face, had a wheelchair ramp installed on the\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 
 front porch. He also fitted handles in the bathroom by the toilet and in the shower to aid my mobility.
\par 
\par \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 
The rehabilitation center equipped me with a brand new wheelchair, a shower bench, and a raised toilet seat. These tools were meant to ease my transition back home. However, when I wheeled inside my house, I was overcome with a sense of helplessness. I lo
\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 oked around, my gaze lost in the distance, and I found myself at a loss. I had only one functioning arm and barely a second leg. Even the simple act of\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37  eating had become a chore.
\par 
\par \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 
I spent countless hours in front of the television, eating, and sinking deeper into depression. There was absolutely nothing to do. I felt trapped within my own mind, my body a prison that I could not escape from. Getting out of bed was a monumental task,
\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 
 requiring the assistance of at least two people. My wife had to help me with the most basic of tasks, including using the restroom. I had a catheter implanted almost the entire time I was in the hospital, so holding my bladder 
\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 
longer than thirty minutes at a time was next to impossible. I would usually scream, " Lisa, I gottsta pee!," wait for her to let out a massive exhale, then bring my urinal in so I could let out my own massive exhale from releasing my bladder It was also 
\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 a massive undertaking to get me into the shower, so she usually resorted to giving me a sponge bath. She would also bring me my toothbrush }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af37\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f37\fs22\lang9\langfe1033\kerning0\langnp9\insrsid8152332 \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 and}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af37\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \f37\fs22\lang9\langfe1033\kerning0\langnp9\insrsid10578587 \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 
 toothpaste to brush in bed. I would squeeze the toothpaste into my mouth, brush,  and typically just swallow what \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 was left.
\par 
\par \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 
One of the strangest experiences was not being able to wiggle my toes on my left leg. It was an eerie sensation, being able to feel everything, yet unable to move anything on that side of my body. This was my new reality, a reality I was struggling to com
\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 e to terms with. But I knew I had to face it, one day at a time. This was just the beginning of my journey towards recovery.
\par 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af37\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f37\fs32\lang9\langfe1033\kerning0\langnp9\insrsid10578587 \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 The Second Phase: Life After a Stroke (3-6 months)}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af37\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f37\fs32\lang9\langfe1033\kerning0\langnp9\insrsid10578587 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af37\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \f37\fs22\lang9\langfe1033\kerning0\langnp9\insrsid10578587 
\par \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 In April of 2019, I welcomed my first and only son into the world. W\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 
e named him Nicholas Junior, after me, of course. His arrival was a blessing, giving me so much more to live for. He came at exactly the right time for me, although it was challenging trying to care for a baby while also focusing on my rehabilitation. My 
\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 wife and parents stepped up, helping me care for him. My 19 year old stepdaughter also lived with us, and she was a huge help.
\par 
\par \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 I spent months living the monotonous life of a disabled stroke survivor, trying to find my purpose. I would do exercises in my be\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 
d and wheelchair, trying to strengthen my leg and ankle. Since I was seated 99% of the time, all I would do was eat and drink cokes. No one is perfect, and i'm no exception. At this point, my depression had gotten so bad that I couldn't have cared less ab
\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 
out my health. Due to this, I quickly balooned up to 320 pounds. This only set me back because it was just that much harder to get up and exercise. Afterall, a body in motion stays in motion, yet I was frozen and FAT.  I'll admit, at this point in my life
\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 , \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 I was doing the bare minimum. Every time I got angry at myself, I would just think of my son and that I'm still here for a reason.
\par 
\par \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 After a few months, the rehab facility allowed me back in, because that\hich\f37 \rquote \loch\f37 
s what my insurance would approve. This is where I would get laser focused on my recovery. This time, I had a huge, white AFO that resembled a big white cast on my left leg. My foot w\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 
ould fit in and it helped keep my ankle straight. I requested my friend, Michael, again. He came in with a huge smile, pumped\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37  I was back. It was great seeing a familiar face, and a guy so pumped I was there again.
\par 
\par \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37  }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af37\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f37\fs32\lang9\langfe1033\kerning0\langnp9\insrsid10578587 \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 The Road to Recovery: Embracing the Challenge}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af37\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f37\fs22\lang9\langfe1033\kerning0\langnp9\insrsid10578587 
\par \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 
This time, my journey towards recovery was different. I was able to practice walking without the constant fear of my ankle rolling. Michael, my therapist, no longer needed to fully support my affected side when I tried to walk. My balance was still far fr
\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 om perfect, so we practiced with a hemi walker, taking small, cautious steps.
\par 
\par \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 We also worked on practical skills like c\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 limbing stairs, getting in and out of the car, and performing stretches on the therapy table. Michael\hich\f37 \rquote \loch\f37 
s upbeat personality was infectious, and he always found a way to lift my spirits when I felt down. We were serious about my recovery, but we also made s\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 
ure to inject some laughter into our sessions. This balance of hard work and humor greatly contributed to my overall sense of well-being and progress.
\par 
\par \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 In Occupational Therapy, my arm was still unresponsive, so we focused on a variety of exercises. We did l\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 
ots of stretches, solved puzzles, practiced getting in and out of bed, brushed teeth, cooked, and performed any living exercise they could think up. Each activity was a challenge, but also a step towards regaining my independence.
\par 
\par \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 In Speech Therapy, there wasn\hich\f37 \rquote \loch\f37 
t too much to do because, thankfully, my cognitive abilities were still sharp. We practiced memory games, critical thinking exercises, swallowing exercises, and enunciation games. Each small victory in these sessions was a te
\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 stament to my resilience \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 and determination.
\par 
\par \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 
After therapy, I would retreat to the sanctuary of my room. And yes, you guessed it, I would eat. The staff was incredibly kind, and I wanted to make the most of my stay, so it seemed like every twenty minutes, I was ordering a sandwich or soda. I also wa
\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 tched a ton of television and played with my phone with my unaffected hand. I got lucky that the stroke affected my off hand.
\par 
\par \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 One of my pastimes was playing Clash Royale, an addicting game where the idea is to build an army of troops, f\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 ight through the opposing player\hich\f37 \rquote \loch\f37 
s troops, destroy their towers and get to their king. This game was beneficial for me because I knew it was exercising my brain and making me think. Anything I could do to improve was the name of the game.
\par 
\par \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 
Although I was battling severe depression, I made sure to do what I needed to do to get a little better every day, especially while in rehab. Still, a part of me craved some caffeine and an energy shot. It was a small reminder of my old life, a life I was
\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37  determined to re\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 claim, one step at a time.
\par 
\par \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 
This time when I went home, I went home a little better. I could back up the ramp, through the front door, all by myself. I could also wheel into the bathroom and brush my own teeth. That was an amazing moral victory. I would squeeze some toothpaste into 
\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 
my mouth with one arm, and brush my own teeth. Transferring onto the bed was a little easier. This time it only took one person helping me. I still had the raised toilet seat, but it was easier to get on and off. Wiping was still
\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37  a chore though because I still couldn't reach.
\par 
\par \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 
Even showering was simpler. No more sponge baths for this guy. The shower bench fit halfway in the shower, and half out. We would put a towel down by the tub to soak up the water. My dad also installed a portable shower head to make it easier to rinse off
\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 . We used a shower sponge and body wash while bathing. I also had a suction cup handle in the shower to stand up and get those hard to reach areas. 
\par 
\par \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 I could tell rehab was working. This motivated me, because\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 
 my goal was to get as close to 100% as I could. Home health then came out. I was on Medicaid. They would support physical therapy, but not occupational therapy, so I had to take what I could. My arm was still totally paralyzed anyway, so I focused on my 
\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 leg. They would come out twice a week and work with me on my walking. It was tough to get out of bed sometimes, but I would always manage to do so.
\par 
\par \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 My son was growing like a weed, and every time I got down on myself, I would tell myself I\hich\f37 \rquote \loch\f37 m still alive, an\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 
d my son still has a father. This was the second phase of my journey, a journey that was far from over, but one that I was determined to see through to the end.
\par 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af37\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f37\fs22\lang9\langfe1033\kerning0\langnp9\insrsid10578587 \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 (9-12 months)}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af37\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \f37\fs22\lang9\langfe1033\kerning0\langnp9\insrsid10578587 
\par \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 
As the months rolled on, I found myself returning to rehabilitation. This time, I was equipped with a bespoke black AFO that could be worn over any shoe of my choosing, provided it had a small adaptive piece for the brace to fit. My ankle had gained stren
\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 gth, yet it remained weak enough to necessitate the brace whenever I was not in m\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 y wheelchair.
\par 
\par \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 
In rehab, I sought out the same therapist and decided to ramp up the intensity of my sessions. Seated in my wheelchair, I engaged in a series of ankle and calf strengthening exercises, coupled with increased walking. Walking proved to be the most benefici
\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 al exercise, enhancing my stamina and engaging all the muscles required for ambulation.
\par 
\par \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 My balance improved significantly, allowing me to progress to a single-pronged cane. We even experimented with removing my brace and walking in short spu\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 
rts, as long as my ankle could withstand it.
\par 
\par \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 
During therapy, we introduced a device known as a Bioness. This device utilized electromagnetic pulses to stimulate the nerves in my leg, triggering them to fire. With the Bioness, I could walk without the need for a brace.
\par 
\par \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 
We also incorporated a robotic-like machine into my therapy. Strapped into a harness, the machine would mimic my walking motion, stride for stride. Initially, it was challenging due to my lingering balance issues, and the unfamiliar sensatio
\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 n of my body moving in ways it hadn\hich\f37 \rquote \loch\f37 t in a long time. However, it felt incredibly rewarding knowing I was regaining my proper walking form.
\par 
\par \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 We dedicated substantial time to this machine, even incorporating small hurdles to practice walking over, in addition to the usual stair climbing and car ingress and egress routines.
\par 
\par \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 During this period, I began to notice small improvements in my arm. In Occupational Therapy, we would lay a towel on a table and I would move my hand across it as much as possible. Excite\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 
d about the progress in my arm, I performed all the exercises I could at home. These included shrugs, arm lifts, table arm motions, and essentially moving my arm in any possible direction. We also incorporated a lot of stretching and range of motion exerc
\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 ises.
\par 
\par \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 
Speech Therapy was no longer necessary this time around. The movement in my arm started in my shoulder and gradually extended to my tricep. By this time, my son had transitioned from crawling to walking, which kept me busy.
\par 
\par \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 When Christmas arrived, I\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37  was able to walk into my parents\hich\f37 \rquote \loch\f37 \hich\f37 
 house for Christmas Dinner. Santa gifted my son an iPad for gaming, which he affectionately referred to as his \'93\loch\f37 \hich\f37 Guy\'94\loch\f37 
. Home Health would visit a couple of times a week, during which I would perform my regular exercises an\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 d walk as far as I could.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af37\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f37\fs22\lang9\langfe1033\kerning0\langnp9\insrsid10578587 \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 12 months+}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af37\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \f37\fs22\lang9\langfe1033\kerning0\langnp9\insrsid10578587 
\par \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 Upon my return to rehab, I was brimming with confidence. However, I was informed that Michael, my usual therapist, was relocating from Fort Smith to Little Rock for the same position. This news was disheartening due to m
\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 y familiarity with him, and it triggered my anxiety. I had to adjust to a new therapist, but not before saying goodbye to Michael and exchanging numbers.
\par 
\par \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 
Over the ensuing months, I worked with various therapists. While they were all excellent, none resonated with me quite like Michael did. I believe that establishing a comfortable rapport with a therapist who understands your body almost as well as you do 
\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 is crucial. They can assist in devising new exercises and determining what works best for you.
\par 
\par \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 A full\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 
 year had elapsed since the stroke. It was a moment for introspection and assessment of my progress. I was acutely aware that each stroke, and consequently each recovery, was unique. Therefore, I had to devise my own recovery plan, refraining from compari
\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 ng my progress with others. I had regained a significant portion of my mobility, yet I remained reliant on my wheelchair.
\par 
\par \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 At home, I was determined to maintain the progress I had made during therapy. I adopted a rigorous approach to my home exercises, espe\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 
cially when the home health team visited. They would encourage me to stand at my kitchen island, transitioning out of my wheelchair to perform various exercises. The exercises I engaged in included:
\par 
\par \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 Calf raises 20 reps x 3h
\par \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 Knee lifts 20 reps x 3
\par \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 Leg circles 20 x 3
\par \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 Side leg raises 20 x 3
\par \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 Ghost squats 20 reps x 3
\par \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 Lunges 20 reps x 3
\par \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 Directly followed by walking for 5 minutes. 
\par \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 Upon my return to therapy, I was introduced to a new therapist, Rob. Rob was a relaxed individual, yet he maintained a serious commit\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 
ment to recovery. We engaged in the same exercises I had been doing at home, but I found the structured environment of Encompass beneficial. It held me accountable and provided a framework for my recovery. Stroke recovery hinges on motivation, positivity,
\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37  and consistency, which is precisely why I returned as frequently as my insurance permitted.
\par 
\par \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 In Occupational Therapy, I met Tony. My previous therapist, Michael, was no longer there. Tony, with his large beard and dry sense of humor, would occasionally eng\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 
age in light-hearted banter with me. Given that some movement had returned to my shoulder, we focused on shrugs and extensive stretching during individual sessions. Group sessions were dedicated to weightlifting. I would strap a weight around my affected 
\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 arm and hold a dumbbell with my other arm. Our routine included shrugs, shoulder presses, curls, tricep extensions, and chest pushes. Since I only had use of my shoulder, my exercises were predominantly one-arm lifts.

\par 
\par \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 Post-therapy, I would retreat to my ro\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 
om to watch TV and play on my phone. This time also served as a period of rest and an opportunity to engage in friendly banter with the staff. With my relationship with Lisa teetering on the brink, these interactions provided a welcome distraction. Upon r
\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 eturning home, I would resume the routine of my wheelchair. I would wheel myself out the door, down the ramp to my front porch, and spend my downtime smoking cigarettes and watching television.
\par 
\par \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 As the second year drew to a close, it was time for self-evalu\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 \hich\f37 
ation. While some things had changed, others remained the same. I could discern improvement, but not to the extent I had hoped. I found myself questioning, \'93\loch\f37 \hich\f37 Could I have exerted more effort to maximize my recovery?\'94\loch\f37 
 Despite this, surrender was not an opti\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 on. I had to persist in my efforts to improve. That\hich\f37 \rquote \loch\f37 
s when I noticed my wife engrossed in TikTok. She was constantly watching videos on the platform. I used to tease her about it, but then it occurred to me - could I leverage TikTok as a catalyst for my r\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 ec
\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 overy? An intriguing thought indeed.
\par 
\par 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af37\afs32 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f37\fs32\lang9\langfe1033\kerning0\langnp9\insrsid10578587 \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 From Wheelchair to Independence: A Tale of Triumph}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af37\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f37\fs22\lang9\langfe1033\kerning0\langnp9\insrsid10578587 
\par \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 
For two and a half years, my universe was bound by the confines of a wheelchair, a device that has been a beacon of independence for individuals with mobility challenges for centuries. This marvel of modern engineering was both my ally and my adversary, s
\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 erving as a constant reminder of my stroke.
\par 
\par \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 My mission was unambiguous: to fortify my ankle to the point where I could abandon my Ankle Foot Orthosis (AFO) and walk u\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 
naided. This was no minor undertaking. It demanded countless hours of physical therapy exercises such as ghost squats, calf raises, knee lifts, side leg raises, and lunges. These exercises, recommended for stroke recovery, are known to enhance physical fi
\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 tness, walking ability, and balance.
\par 
\par \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 Despite the progress in my leg strength, my ankle remained a formidable challenge. Resolute to conquer this obstacle, I dedicated my spare time in my recliner to performing toe raises and foot circles at the ankle, alte
\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 
rnating between clockwise and counter-clockwise movements. I would even wrap the elastic band around my foot and practice toe raises and extensions to cure my drop foot. After a few months of this rigorous regimen, I felt it was time to embark on the next
\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37  phase of my journey.
\par 
\par \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 
I pushed my wheelchair aside and took my first unassisted steps. I moved from chair to chair, without the aid of an AFO, cane, brace, or any other support. The progress was gradual and the journey strenuous, but I was achieving it! I 
\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 was walking unaided.
\par 
\par \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 The path to recovery was slow, but every step forward was a victory. I began by walking from room to room, then from my house to my car, and eventually, from my house to my parents\hich\f37 \rquote \loch\f37 
 house, a distance of about 150 yards. The uneven terrain made the journey\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37  more challenging, but I kept my eyes on the ground, avoiding any pitfalls, and ensuring my safety.
\par 
\par \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 After a few months, I felt ready to attempt a journey inside Walmart. Until then, I had been using an electric cart. I finally took th\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 
e plunge, exited the car, and cautiously entered the store. It was nerve-wracking as I felt the gaze of everyone on me with each step I took. I was terrified of falling. I would grab a shopping cart for support and commence my journey. Step by step, I wou
\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 ld quickly grab bread and groceries to shorten the trip and exit the store.
\par 
\par \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 As the weeks passed, things started to get easier. The pace of shopping slowed down, and I grew more comfortable. My endurance improved, and I was able to shop for longer periods. \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 If you
\hich\f37 \rquote \loch\f37 
re feeling nervous or anxious, the best strategy is to immerse yourself in the situation and strive to feel comfortable. Despite my tension, I forced myself to go through the shopping experience, and I believe I emerged a stronger person because of
\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37  it. I confronted my fears, as I always do, and emerged victorious!
\par 
\par \hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 Recovering from a stroke is not just a physical journey, but an emotional one as well. Stroke survivors often grapple with feelings of irritability, forgetfulness, carelessness, inattentio\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 
n, or confusion. Emotions such as fear, frustration, anger, grief, sadness, anxiety, and depression are also common. However, with perseverance and a positive attitude, recovery is achievable. Remember, every step, no matter how small, is a step forward. 
\hich\af37\dbch\af31505\loch\f37 Keep moving, keep striving, and keep believing in yourself. Your journey is unique to you, and every victory, no matter how small, is a testament to your strength and resilience.
